Mm-hmm.

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:31.259
Yeah, since...

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:32.369
So these are Students yeah.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:32.780
Mm-hmm.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:37.619
So since, you know, English tests are
really important in Japan, so that's

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:43.100
why I teach test taking strategies
or, uh, you know, sometimes I, I

00:01:43.104 --> 00:01:44.699
tell them how to study English.

00:01:46.679 --> 00:01:50.820
So to do well on TOEIC, I
presume there are two components.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:56.290
There is actually improving your
level in the language: vocabulary,

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.330
your grammar or whatever.

00:01:57.615 --> 00:02:02.475
And then the other is understanding,
you know, having a strategy on

00:02:02.475 --> 00:02:04.104
how to do well in the exams.

00:02:04.164 --> 00:02:07.935
Can you share with us some of
the strategies for improving

00:02:08.055 --> 00:02:10.695
English and some of the strategies
for doing well in the exam?

00:02:11.145 --> 00:02:11.685
Okay.

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:15.255
So basically, yeah to get a good score...

00:02:15.555 --> 00:02:20.325
yeah, of course you have to improve your
English level, right, technique up to

00:02:20.355 --> 00:02:26.475
a certain level, but, uh,  even native
speakers cannot get the perfect score

00:02:26.505 --> 00:02:30.405
easily because, because they have...

00:02:30.405 --> 00:02:34.395
yeah, especially the reading part,
you have to find like several

00:02:34.395 --> 00:02:36.615
pieces of information, you know?

00:02:36.705 --> 00:02:40.795
So you can't just answer
quickly all the questions.

00:02:40.815 --> 00:02:46.575
So that's the yeah one part of the, like
one really difficult part of the test.

00:02:47.775 --> 00:02:49.620
And then, so...

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:50.970
Um, yeah, go ahead.

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:55.200
So you gather, uh, several pieces of
information and you have to figure

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.660
out the synonyms or yeah, so like the
synonyms are really, really important.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:09.690
Mm-hmm , uh, in terms of, you know,
finding bits of information in a text,

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:13.020
for example, whether you're reading
or listening but especially if you're

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:20.065
reading, I would imagine the ability
to read fast  is important because

00:03:20.065 --> 00:03:23.445
you may have to read more than once
the same text before you find whatever

00:03:23.445 --> 00:03:24.725
information they're looking for.

00:03:24.725 --> 00:03:26.024
Mm-hmm right.

00:03:26.204 --> 00:03:26.625
Actually.

00:03:26.625 --> 00:03:26.864
Yeah.

00:03:26.864 --> 00:03:32.144
Actually most people cannot finish
this test becasue it's yeah.

00:03:32.144 --> 00:03:39.404
You have to read yeah a bunch of  long
texts and then, but, so, so basically

00:03:39.404 --> 00:03:45.600
I recommend people, uh, try to find
the necessary information, not try

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.210
to read, uh, all of the passages.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:55.560
So it's a strategy of sort of
skimming the text in order to zoom

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:59.790
in on specific bits of information
so that you can answer the question.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:00.210
Right?

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.330
And so you, what you teach, you
teach a technique for doing that?

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:07.680
Mm-hmm yeah, kind of, and then skimming
and the, uh, scanning are really

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:15.090
important and that's what ETS, uh, the
people who make the test think, but the

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:21.410
problem is we don't learn that in Japan.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:28.695
We just analyze the sentence structure
and then read, read the sentence.

00:04:28.755 --> 00:04:32.955
Uh, and then, then translate
the sentence into Japanese.

00:04:33.495 --> 00:04:38.685
So of course it is very time
consuming, so that's why more Japanese

00:04:38.685 --> 00:04:40.635
people cannot finish this test.

00:04:41.865 --> 00:04:42.225
Okay.

00:04:42.225 --> 00:04:47.810
But to be fair, when you are reading in a
foreign language it's a lot more difficult

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:52.410
to skim and, and find, you know, important
bits of information when you're reading.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.930
In your own language it's
like instant meaning, right?

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:58.430
I mean, I can look at the paragraph
of English and I, I see it all.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.070
If I'm learning, if I'm in
another language, I'm kind of

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.855
going word for word for word to
try and figure out what it means.

00:05:05.255 --> 00:05:10.950
It's not fair to expect the non-native
speaker to have the same ability to

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:16.140
skim or scan as the native speaker,
doesn't matter what the language is.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.180
So...

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:18.430
yeah.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.560
That's yeah, you can say that,
but, but what, what they say

00:05:22.565 --> 00:05:24.690
is like, like the test...

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:31.140
so the basically TOEIC tests your ability
to live in the English speaking country.

00:05:31.995 --> 00:05:34.454
yeah, basically the United States.

00:05:34.545 --> 00:05:42.090
And then, yeah, maybe in other English
speaking countries, such as UK, Australia.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.405
So that's why they ask us to
do just like native speakers.

00:05:48.885 --> 00:05:53.625
But of course, of course, that's really,
really difficult for non native speaker.

00:05:55.065 --> 00:05:55.545
Right.

00:05:55.635 --> 00:05:55.875
It's...

00:05:55.875 --> 00:05:58.725
it doesn't matter whether it's a
Japanese person learning English or

00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.195
an English speaker learning Japanese.

00:06:00.555 --> 00:06:05.715
It's the same: reading in another writing
system, reading in another language it,

00:06:05.715 --> 00:06:10.065
it's not, it's not that instant meaning
that it is in, in your own language.

00:06:10.305 --> 00:06:14.145
Unless you have had so much exposure
to the language and I get back to my

00:06:14.145 --> 00:06:19.185
favorite theme of lots of listening and
reading, while the native speaker has

00:06:19.185 --> 00:06:22.845
had millions and millions of hours of
listening to the language whether it

00:06:22.845 --> 00:06:25.015
be a Japanese or English and reading.

00:06:25.335 --> 00:06:28.785
So you, as a learner, you haven't
had that exposure, but the more

00:06:28.785 --> 00:06:32.835
exposure you have, the closer you
get to the level of the native.

00:06:32.835 --> 00:06:33.995
Right, exactly.

00:06:34.205 --> 00:06:34.395
Right.

00:06:35.475 --> 00:06:39.270
And the problem is that, you know, the
test is getting harder and harder...

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:47.205
cuz you know, a lot of test takers
learn like useful test taking strategies

00:06:47.505 --> 00:06:49.785
and they get, get good scores.

00:06:50.575 --> 00:06:52.965
And then the test will become harder.

00:06:54.375 --> 00:06:54.975
Right.

00:06:56.415 --> 00:06:59.415
One of the things, if you don't mind
me, me saying, so I've always found it

00:06:59.415 --> 00:07:04.935
strange that in Japan people take TOEIC
a lot, they take it often, they take it

00:07:04.935 --> 00:07:12.285
3, 4, 5.And when I last looked at it, it
seemed like for most universities or for

00:07:12.525 --> 00:07:14.875
employment, you need quite a high score.

00:07:15.675 --> 00:07:18.510
Which like, over 700 or, or
something like that, right?

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:18.910
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:18.915 --> 00:07:24.930
And so if you test and you would get only
350 or 400, that's almost kind of useless.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:25.320
Right?

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:31.380
So my question has always been
why wouldn't a person wait until

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.590
they're confident that they have
a fairly decent level in the

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.480
language before taking the test?

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:40.450
Why take the test to confirm
that your level is low.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:44.070
Uh, you know, if, if I were a Japanese
person, I wouldn't take the test

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.460
until I was fairly confident that I
would have at least 750, but a lot

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.440
of people in Japan end up with like
300, 400 and they take the test over

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:53.910
and over again, they get 300, 400.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:08:00.360
Why not wait until, you know, you're
gonna get 750, or is that not realistic?

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:00.810
I Don't know.

00:08:01.469 --> 00:08:04.650
I think the issue is
motivation, motivation.

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.960
Like if we don't have
tests, we don't study.

00:08:07.260 --> 00:08:07.739
Right.

00:08:07.739 --> 00:08:09.239
So  okay.

00:08:09.239 --> 00:08:10.215
That's a problem.

00:08:11.745 --> 00:08:13.260
So they study for the test.

00:08:13.289 --> 00:08:14.219
Yeah, exactly.

00:08:14.250 --> 00:08:15.450
So that's kind of sad.

00:08:15.979 --> 00:08:17.159
TOEIC could...

00:08:17.270 --> 00:08:21.980
TOEIC stands for test of English for
international communication, but...

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.870
but yeah, in fact, yeah, we just
study for TOEIC, you know, so, which

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.070
has only listening and reading.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:35.640
So the average person who takes
TOEIC and takes it quite a few times,

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.880
like, do most of them eventually
improve up to a level of 750 800?

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.530
Or do most of them kind of
stay at that 400, 450 level?

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:48.990
Uh, it depends on the person, but like
if the person likes the test, if, if

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:53.730
the person is into the test, of course
their score usually improves a lot.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:58.800
But like, if you don't like
English or tests, you know,

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:00.460
of course they just stay...

00:09:01.180 --> 00:09:02.290
remain the same.

00:09:03.610 --> 00:09:04.150
Right.

00:09:04.490 --> 00:09:06.310
So, so it's as much a...

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:06.810
yeah.

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:11.070
Oh, so it's like once they get
the, you know, the, their, uh,

00:09:11.075 --> 00:09:13.080
score like satisfactory score.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:13.590
Yeah.

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:16.380
Most people basically
quit studying English.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.760
So of course their level
will go down again.

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:23.280
So what's that minimum level
that they need in order to not

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:24.330
